CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
static_assert condition could not be evaluated
dropped the body
--
Two defects.  A constexpr object of class type was a fold-away macro without
a value: at namespace scope it was never initialized (`constexpr S g;` with a
default member initializer read 0 at run time -- wrong verdict), and no
member read or member function call on it folded in a static_assert.  Such an
object is now a const object ([dcl.constexpr]/1) whose constant value is
computed by the constant evaluator (default member initializers, constexpr
constructors, value-initialized temporaries) and recorded, and the evaluator
binds `this` to that value for a member function call.  Note: a
static_assert at namespace scope that cannot be evaluated is skipped silently;
the block-scope ones are checked and warn.  Non-vacuous: the last assertion
states a wrong value and must FAIL.
